---
name: openscad
description: Use when creating, editing, rendering, or iterating on OpenSCAD (.scad) 3D models, exporting/viewing STL files, or previewing SCAD geometry with the openscad CLI. Covers headless PNG rendering, looking at the render to iterate, cross-sections, dimension checks, and fixing the model.
---

# OpenSCAD modeling & iteration

Drive OpenSCAD from the CLI. Render previews, look at them, refine.
macOS binary: `/opt/homebrew/bin/openscad` (check `which openscad`).

## Loop

1. Render PNG (no GUI):
   ```sh
   openscad --render --autocenter --viewall --imgsize=1000,900 -o /tmp/model.png model.scad
   ```
   `--render` = full CGAL evaluation. `--viewall` fits frame. Check output for
   `Status: NoError`, plus any `ERROR`/`WARNING`.
2. Look at it: Read the PNG. It returns as an attachment. Never guess the
   shape — render and view.
3. Edit `model.scad`. Repeat from 1.
4. Export STL when the shape is right:
   ```sh
   openscad -o model.stl model.scad
   ```

## Show the user

Render angled + side + top, open all (macOS). Do this after each round unless
told otherwise.
```sh
# angled
openscad --render --autocenter --viewall --imgsize=1000,900 -o /tmp/model.png model.scad
# side (orthographic)
openscad --render --projection=o --autocenter --viewall \
  --camera=0,-300,27,90,0,0,300 --imgsize=700,900 -o /tmp/model_side.png model.scad
# top (orthographic)
openscad --render --projection=o --autocenter --viewall \
  --camera=0,0,50,0,0,0,0 --imgsize=800,800 -o /tmp/model_top.png model.scad
open /tmp/model.png /tmp/model_side.png /tmp/model_top.png
```

## Cross-section (find grooves, holes, voids)

Export STL first. Then:
```sh
cat > /tmp/xsec.scad <<'EOF'
difference() {
	import("model.stl");
	translate([0,-100,0]) cube([200,200,200]);
}
EOF
openscad --render --projection=o --viewall --autocenter \
  --camera=0,-300,27,90,0,0,300 --imgsize=700,900 -o /tmp/xsec.png /tmp/xsec.scad
open /tmp/xsec.png
```
Top view: `--camera=0,0,50,0,0,0,0`.

## Check real dimensions

Measure the STL, not the source. ASCII STL here; parse vertices:
```sh
python3 - <<'PY'
import re
t = open("model.stl").read()
v = [tuple(map(float, m)) for m in re.findall(r"vertex\s+(\S+)\s+(\S+)\s+(\S+)", t)]
xs=[a for a,_,_ in v]; ys=[b for _,b,_ in v]; zs=[c for _,_,c in v]
print("X %.2f  Y %.2f  Z %.2f" % (max(xs)-min(xs), max(ys)-min(ys), max(zs)-min(zs)))
PY
```

## Conventions

- Use as few constants as possible. Derive values from those (`base_r / 2`, `base_r - ring`).
- No comments narrating past state or filler comments.
- Keep manifold: `Status: NoError`, no warnings. Union primitives so they
  overlap/fill — no grooves, holes, or cavities. Verify with a cross-section.
- `$fa`/`$fs` at real size give smoother curves than scaling up a tiny model.
